  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/5/5f/Fantastic_Four_Annual_Vol_1_1.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The story opens with Namor at his coronation (in Atlantis), having finally found his long-lost undersea kingdom, and he vows retribution on the \"insolent human race\" for its crimes.&nbsp; Lady Dorma and Warlord Krang are present, the former happy to see her \"loved one\" again, while the latter (Krang) reminds her she is pledged to him!&nbsp; Dorma is having <i>none</i> of it -- Namor is her \"true love.\"&nbsp; Krang inwardly seethes at Namor's return.\n\nBack at FF HQ, we get the standard three pages of Ben and Johnny fighting each other (interrupting Ben in the middle of writing a scathing response to the Yancy Street Gang's latest missive).&nbsp; Reed of course shows up to put a stop to it, rolling Ben up, who hollers \"You can't do this to the idol of millions!\"&nbsp; Sue's only concern is that her \"expensive Dior and Saks Fifth Avenue dresses\" are ruined -- \"<b>Men</b>!&nbsp; You're <i>all</i> beasts!\"\n\nReed says they're all on edge and need a vacation.&nbsp; He says he heard one experimental atomic sub encountered a sea monster in the middle of the Atlantic, while a commercial jet spotted another giant sea creature in the area.&nbsp; He suggests a cruise to that general vicinity as a way of combining business with pleasure.&nbsp; Alicia arrives just then and insists on coming along.\n\nIt's not long before Johnny spots a sea monster, and the four investigate.&nbsp; Different fish are used to surround them in air bubbles and are dragged down into the depths.&nbsp; There, they are greeted by Namor, who informs them that his \"tele-fish\" spied on them, actually transmitting \"visual impressions\" from his spines (<i>convenient</i>).\n\nTurns out Namor was behind the appearances of the sea monsters.&nbsp; He <i>wanted</i> the FF to find him so he could deliver an ultimatum to the United Nations that from now on, the seas belong to him.&nbsp; All ships that venture into it will be turned back toward land.&nbsp; No aircraft either!&nbsp; He then sends the FF back to deliver his message, and Reed is impressed with the advanced technology that is used to return them home.\n\nReed soon addresses the UN (where we hilariously see one Communist member banging his shoe on the table voting \"Nyet! Nyet! Nyet!\")&nbsp; But Reed anticipated resistance to the very grave threat that Namor and his undersea army represents, so a Dr. G. W. Falton (an authority on undersea life) addresses them to explain Namor's origin.&nbsp; He holds up a skull, saying it is \"Homo Mermanus, or Man of the Sea!\"&nbsp; He explains the parallel evolution of the undersea civilization, which became the legend of Atlantis, which largely was left alone.&nbsp; But in 1920, man made his first recorded contact with Homo Mermanus aboard the ice-breaker \"Oracle\" commanded by Captain Leonard McKenzie near the South Pole.&nbsp; Their destruction of a large iceberg rocked an undersea settlement, and the king sent his daughter, Princess Fen to investigate.&nbsp; It seems the people of Atlantis can survive in the air for about 5 hours at a time.&nbsp; She is discovered, and over the course of time, McKenzie and Fen fall in love and are married.&nbsp; The King, wondering about his daughter, dispatches a war party to engage the ship, and McKenzie is killed, but Fen had already become pregnant with what turned out to be Namor, able to breathe both air <i>and</i> water.&nbsp; He sums up his speech by saying \"This was Prince Namor, the Sub-Mariner, possibly the first mutant of our time!\" (and the only true amphibian on Earth)\n\nReed then catches them up, pointing out that his undersea home was \"accidentally destroyed by an H-bomb test at sea!\"&nbsp; And he adds that the surface world must be resolute and \"fight him until he can threaten us no more!\"&nbsp; And then we learn that Dr. G. W. Falton is actually the Sub-Mariner in disguise, who upon hearing Reed's comments declares war upon the human race!\n\nSuddenly, NYC is under attack -- Atlantis is invading!&nbsp; And by afternoon, Namor's forces take over the city.&nbsp; They try to encase FF HQ in cement, but the Torch counter-attacks, but they find that \"Namor's legions are attacking <i>everywhere</i>!\"&nbsp; Ben goes out and rounds up some of Namor's forces for Reed to study, and Reed notices the water-filled helmets.&nbsp; It's only a matter of coming up with a way to evaporate that water from their helmets.\"\n\nNamor stands in Central Park and gives out his edicts as conqueror, even going so far as to point out that they need to make sure \"humans never again gain control of Earth's surface!\"&nbsp; And the military is afraid to attack since there are 9 million people in the city they don't want to accidentally harm.&nbsp; But Reed is busy finishing his device.&nbsp; Sure enough, the helmet water begins to evaporate, driving the army back into the ocean.\n\nThe invasion is thus halted by Mr. Fantastic, and Namor is livid and strikes back, attacking the FF, but Namor grabs Sue and flies away.&nbsp; Retreating to the oceans now with his prisoner, Namor vows (with Lady Dorma at his side) that he has only <i>begun</i> to fight.&nbsp; He attacks Johnny, hovering above their location, and Reed and Ben soon join him.&nbsp; A couple of pages of all-out action ensue, while Sue below pleads for both Namor and the FF to stop.&nbsp; Krang and Lady Dorma then realize that Namor actually loves Sue, and Dorma will have none of <i>that</i>!&nbsp; She attempts to destroy Sue, but Krang points out that if Namor loves the human, Dorma is free to be his!&nbsp; She smashes one of the portholes so as to let water rush in and drown Sue, who attempts to kick herself back to the surface.&nbsp; But she gets tangled and is only noticed in time by the battling Ben and Namor -- she needs hospitalization immediately!&nbsp; Namor returns to the undersea craft from which Sue escaped and orders everyone out.&nbsp; Dorma is incensed!&nbsp; Namor helping the <i>enemy</i>?\n\nHe returns to the surface to take Sue to the hospital.&nbsp; Reed knows it's no trick, \"for Namor, in his own strange way, loves Sue as much as we do!\"&nbsp; They get back to Manhattan a couple of hours later, having to take the time to repair the damage Namor caused to their own craft.&nbsp; But they find Sue in the hospital, recovering nicely.\n\nThey find Namor walking through an angry crowd, who jeer at his failure to take over the surface world.&nbsp; Namor shoves them aside and returns to his craft, only to go home and find everything deserted!&nbsp; The once-adoring kingdom has sensed that Namor has betrayed it by giving his heart to a surface woman.&nbsp; Namor is left all alone -- \"a king without a kingdom... Is there <i>never</i> to be a place for me -- on the surface or in the sea?\"&nbsp; More is promised in future issues of the Fantastic Four!",
  "grade_one_liner": "Ambitious in scale, but unevenly delivered and weakened by familiar beats.\nA memorable attempt that races too hard toward a story that doesn't hold up.",
  "subtitle": "Atlantis Attacks, But Make It Quick",
  "review_text": "[SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=FOR THE RECORD:]\n[BR] This is a review of the first of the three stories found in this magazine.\n[BR] My review on the second story is posted elsewhere.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nWhile this was by no means a classic tale, it was nonetheless a solid -- and <i>lengthy</i> -- one, with reasonable pace, despite a few questionable moments.&nbsp; I believe I can say with some degree of confidence that showing Namor back with his people was a welcome sight, even though one of the first things out of his mouth was how he would \"never rest till the insolent human race has paid for its crimes against our people!\"&nbsp; But... returning him home to Atlantis was bound to happen sooner or later, and including it in a story as vast as this one seemed about right.\n\n[ASSET: right | NotSoRosy | width=13%] Naturally though, Stan and Jack were quick to show that the situation in Atlantis wasn't all rosy, as the introduction of Warlord Krang shows, he, who was pledged to marry to Lady Dorma.&nbsp; And yet, this seems to have been some kind of arrangement that now must take a backseat to the return of Namor the First, for Dorma <i>clearly</i> only has eyes for her now-returned lord, much to the <i>displeasure</i> of Krang.&nbsp; The bottom of page 4 shows the inner thoughts of each major player.\n\nOne wonders if Stan and Jack might have done better to eliminate the standard 2-3 pages of Ben vs. Johnny at the beginning and instead replaced it with a more spaced out sequence later when Namor takes over the city, which seemed to happen in the blink of an eye.&nbsp; Such a shift would have reinforced the gravity of Namor's takeover instead of front‑loading the issue with routine horseplay.\n\nBut... it is an <b><i>annual</i></b>, and extra space was available.&nbsp; The co-creators at least do a decent job transitioning from goofing around to moving the Fantastic Four nearer the imminent threat by using the guise of the vacation getaway at sea on the cruise ship \"Holiday\":\n[ASSET: center | BusinessAndPleasure | width=67%]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nTo <i>nobody's</i> surprise, their vacation is cut short, and with the help of another bunch of crazy fish (especially offensive and silly was the \"tele-fish\" that transmitted visual images to Namor - [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Ugh!]).\n\nStill, once the FF reach the ocean floor, the story finally snaps into its larger geopolitical stakes, where the Sub-Mariner reveals his plan to address the United Nations and deliver an ultimatum.\n[ASSET: center | NamorsPlan | width=67%]\n\nAll of the heretofore slow moving plot serves to get the reader to page 15, which still left <i>plenty of room</i> for more exposition, and Stan and Jack don't disappoint.&nbsp; It's one of the rare Silver Age stretches where exposition feels genuinely ambitious rather than perfunctory, and the threat of a unified, undersea kingdom such as Atlantis is no laughing matter, which Reed tries to explain to the world dignitaries.\n\n[ASSET: left | FirstMutant | width=14%] [ASSET: right | NamorDeclaresWar | width=15%] Pages 15-18 recount an extravagant origin tale for the race of Atlanteans, not to mention going into detail about the doomed romance between Captain Leonard McKenzie and Princess Fen of Atlantis, all near the South Pole, close to the headquarters of the undersea kingdom.&nbsp; From their brief union, Namor, \"the first known mutant,\" was born.\n[BR]\n[BR] But after Reed makes it clear that the exposition was for the purpose of urging the world to \"fight him,\" Namor reveals himself and declares \"war upon the human race!\" (still leaving about half the story for the actual conflict, which I thought was good pacing).\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | InstantTakeover | width=12%] After that, however, things got a little crazy, and by the end of a single page (20), by \"early afternoon,\" Namor and his forces were in \"complete control\" of New York City.\n[BR]\n[BR] (Where are all those \"nuclear warheads\" that we saw back in [LINK:1961:FF_1|FF #1]?&nbsp; I would imagine they might have come in handy right about now!)\n\nFrom here, the narrative begins to spiral, compressing major events into implausibly tiny windows of time.&nbsp; Not only that, but we learn that the underwater kingdom has \"thousands of planes\" that the Human Torch has to try and destroy (page 21).&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=What?]&nbsp; Why would an undersea race build <i>planes</i>?\n\nAnd by the end of page 22, Namor actually stands in Central Park to declare \"martial law!\"&nbsp; (This is plot acceleration that only the Silver Age could deliver with a straight face --and you just have to <i>laugh</i>!)\n\nThe military is shown being hesitant to attack the city, for fear of its 9 million inhabitants being harmed.&nbsp; But I feel the need to remind you that this is the same military that managed to completely <i>evacuate the entire city</i> back in last year's [LINK:1962:FF_4|FF #4] when the Sub-Mariner first appeared and threatened it with his Giganto monster.&nbsp; We can only assume that <i>this </i>time, they were taken off guard.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Or more likely... <i>neither</i> Stan nor Jack remembered this...)]\n\nRegardless, Namor and his invasion force are in <i>complete control</i> of New York City, and things look grim:\n[ASSET: center | CompleteControl | width=67%]\n\nEven though Reed is able to successfully begin evaporating the water out of the helmets of the undersea invasion force (on page 25), it raises the questions below.&nbsp; The contradictions pile up quickly, and the story never attempts to reconcile them.\n[HANG] * Why were the soldiers of Atlantis wearing water-filled helmets in the <i>first place</i> when it was explained earlier in the story that Princess Fen could survive up to five hours outside of the sea?\n[HANG] * Was Fen just <i>special</i> that way?\n[HANG] * Is this a normal ability that all the people of Atlantis share?&nbsp; (Perhaps not, because Namor becomes a mutant.)\n[HANG] * Did Namor inherit some mutant ability from his mother?\n[HANG] * Pages 28, 32 and 33 show that Krang and Dorma have no problem surviving for a time in an air-filled compartment, so why did the members of the invasion force have to return to the water <i>immediately</i>?\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThere wasn't really much left in terms of plot from page 25 onward.&nbsp; Namor takes Sue hostage aboard an undersea craft, and the rest of the Fantastic Four were triggered enough to do endless battle.\n[ASSET: center | BattleIsOn | width=67%]\n\n[ASSET: left | EvilDorma | width=22%] One of the only two twists left was the unexpected behavior of Lady Dorma when faced with the prospect of Namor actually being in love with a surface woman.&nbsp; She actually tries to <i>kill</i> Sue after that, ironically putting Krang in the position of trying to <i>defend</i> Sue so that Namor would be free to have Sue, leaving him free to have Dorma.\n[BR]\n[BR] It was a final, clever wrinkle, injecting a burst of interpersonal drama even if it does little to make either Atlantean sympathetic.\n\n(Incidentally, these pages also show how <i>pathetic</i> Sue's offensive ability is as part of the FF -- all you have to do is tie her hands behind her back, and she's essentially <i>helpless</i>.)\n\n[ASSET: right | SavingAnEnemy | width=28%] And then when Namor returned to his undersea command craft and revealed to Dorma and Krang that he intended to \"save an enemy,\" they both were <i>incensed</i>!&nbsp; He leaves them behind and races Sue to a hospital, where she gets the attention she needs to survive.&nbsp; It's a striking pivot for a character who began the story vowing vengeance on the surface world.\n[BR]\n[BR] The rest of the FF join her \"two hours later\" at Manhattan General and are happy to find her well.\n\n[ASSET: left | LonelyNamor | width=10%] As for Namor, he's forced to swipe aside the jeering crowd in NYC, warning that \"the <i>next</i> time you set eyes upon me, you will all be groveling in the dust.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] The [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved], but Namor's return to Atlantis is a sobering one, since the entire populace has abandoned the city, leaving him a \"king without a kingdom -- a man without a home.\"\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nProviding an origin story for the Sub-Mariner was absolutely necessary, and one can easily point to this story as its greatest strength.&nbsp; Also, it's probably <i>no coincidence</i> that Namor is described as a \"mutant\" on the very same day as the debut issue of the <i>X-Men</i>, where the term \"mutant\" is truly added to the Marvel lexicon.\n\nI might also argue that the re-introduction of Lady Dorma to Silver Age readers was a positive for the book, as was the first appearance of Warlord Krang, though at this point, <i>neither</i> of the two new characters come across as very likable.&nbsp; Yet it seems clear we haven't heard the last from them, which is, in itself, another positive, as it leaves the reader wanting a bit more (always a good thing).\n\nTaken together, these elements nudge the story slightly above mid‑grade -- a good, if uneven, read.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=However...] the crazy fish, planes from an undersea kingdom, and all too quick takeover of Manhattan -- these were difficult to ignore in terms of quality.&nbsp; And let's face it:&nbsp; any reader worth his salt should have suspected the story might come down to Namor's love of Sue -- ultimately leading to his downfall.\n\nNamor being abandoned in the end was admittedly tragic, <i>true</i>, but he still comes across as a villain in this story.&nbsp; What was his first knee-jerk reaction to returning to Atlantis as ruler?&nbsp; I might remind you that it was to order an attack on the \"insolent human race\" above.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=<i>Really</i>?&nbsp; What was the <i>rush</i>?]\n\nAnd at the end, when the attack ultimately failed, why the people of Atlantis go so far as to <i>abandon their own homes</i> instead of simply staying put at the end and telling Namor to get lost?&nbsp; That, too, seems a bridge too far.\n\nIn terms of pacing, I thought Stan and Jack could have dispensed with the early Ben vs. Johnny battle and instead devoted a little more space to the takeover of Manhattan.&nbsp; Both consumed about three pages each, though one can <i>hardly</i> say that they have equal weight in terms of storytelling.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But that's just <i>me</i>...)]\n\n[ASSET: left | WhatAnEgghead | width=15%] As for the characterization, I <i>loved</i> the second-to-last panel of page 13, where Ben says \"What an <i>egg-head</i>!&nbsp; Reed, if they ever strapped ya in the electric chair, you'd probably be explainin' how the blamed thing <i>works</i> while they pulled the switch!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=(<i>Heh</i>, well-done, Stan!)]\n\nIn the final analysis, it's easy to argue that this story was probably too long -- it simply did not need all the real estate allotted to it.&nbsp; The origin was <i>good </i>(though I confess I don't know if this was a retelling of something already revealed in Namor's many Golden Age appearances or actual new material).&nbsp; 37 pages seems like a <i>huge</i> amount of space, and I applaud Stan and Jack for making this first FF annual a spectacle.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text= You can't take anything away from the <i>attempt</i>, which I appreciated.]\n\nStill... despite all the positives, it suffered from pacing issues, and the moment Namor kidnapped Sue on page 27, the ending was essentially telegraphed (though I confess I didn't see Dorma and Krang's very fascinating interactions being what they were, so hats off to the co-creators for <i>that</i> at least).\n\nAlso, the people of Atlantis behave irrationally from start to finish -- embracing Namor too quickly, attacking the surface immediately, and abandoning him just as abruptly.\n\nFrom where I sit:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text= that simply does not compute].",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * There are a few pin-up pages of the FF's villains' gallery, along with a two-page spread featuring \"Questions and Answers About the Fantastic Four\" where they address some of the fans' various questions, which was another nice touch, very much in the same vein as what they did in [LINK:1962:FF_11_ff1|FF #11].&nbsp; (The months that are shown next to each villain seem to correspond with the original <i>cover</i> dates, not the actual publication dates -- but this is pretty typical).\n\n[HANG0] * We learn on these Questions and Answers pages the following interesting details:\n[HANG] - Reed can stretch up to about a hundred yards (before it gets painful).\n[HANG] - Johnny is sixteen years old, a senior in high school (a little <i>young</i> to be a senior), and can fly around 50 mph.\n[HANG] - Sue likes to read romantic novels and isn't sure if she loves Reed or Namor (though see note below...)\n[HANG] - No one really knows why the Thing has only four fingers on each hand, and Ben is too \"frightened to propose\" to Alicia.\n\n[HANG0] * The included diagram of the inside of the Baxter Building shows 6 upper floors on the outside, but only 5 in the cross-section diagram ([SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=Oops!])&nbsp; And if that weren't bad enough, the \"villain\" page showing Dr. Doom shows only <i>four</i>!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=small; text=(Will someone please make up their <i>mind</i>?)]\n\n[HANG0] * The second story in the annual is a retelling of Spider-Man's first encounter with the FF, but <i>with additional detail</i>, which is reviewed elsewhere (and gets its own grade).\n\n[HANG0] * The annual concludes with a reprinted version of the first half of [LINK:1961:FF_1|FF #1], recounting the team's origin, where... <i>sure enough</i>... right there on page 9, it shows Sue saying \"Don't say it, Reed!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=I'm your <b>fiancee</b>!]&nbsp; Where <i>you</i> go, <i>I </i>go!\"\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(So what's the mystery about who she loves?)]\n\n[HANG0] * When all is said and done, there are actually <b>70</b> non-advertisement pages in this published magazine.&nbsp; Adding the three Questions and Answers pages to the main 37-page story gives us 40 pages, or around the size of two full book-length stories, which is where the weight of 2 came from for this review.&nbsp; (The other review of the Spider-Man/FF story is 6 pages, which is around a third of a book, so it gets a .33 weighting -- you'll see this in the monthly summary.)"
  ],
  "letters_page": "Note that there's a <i>great</i> letter about this annual published in FF #23 by Stephen Foston (the last letter published) -- <i>really</i> great letter about how excited he was to pick up the annual he couldn't find anywhere!&nbsp; I loved reading it!",
  "highlight": "The Fantastic Four face the Sub-Mariner in an annual that attacks <i>big</i> but lands exactly as always."
}